  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Friday...\n\nWidespread rain will continue over the next 24 hours. An estimated\n0.75-1.25\" of rain has fallen in a swath extending from Sweet\nGrass County and northeast through northern Custer County.\nLocalized heavier amounts up to 3 inches has occurred within this\nwatch as well. Expect more rain to occur over the next 24 to 30\nhours.\n\nThe root cause of all this reain is an upper level low that is\npositions over southwest Idaho as of noon. Moisture brought in\nwith it the Pacific, as well as a sharp easterly surge of low-\nlevel moisture has PWATs approaching 1.3\" at and east of\nYellowstone County. While widespread rain will persist, the larger\nissue is keeping an eye out for pockets of localized, heavier\nrainfall.\n\nAnalysis shows CAPE up to 500 J/kg across southeast MT this\nafternoon, with modest shear (especially for mid September). These\ningredients combined with the ample ascent provided by the upper\nlow will bring about storm potential through the evening.\nSoundings show long, skinny CAPEs alongside a deep, saturated\nlayer, further indicating the heavy rain potential of these \nstorms. Due to all of this, a rare Moderate risk of Excessive \nRainfall exists from Yellowstone County through Custer and Powder \nRiver counties, meaning that any storm has at least a 40% chance \nof inducing flash flooding. These rainfall rates could be as high \nas 3\" of rain per hour, and the most likely timeframe of these\nstorms occurring is now through 9pm. Do not wait for a storm to \noccur, have a plan ready now in case of possible flash flooding.\nFlood Watch for all of southeastern MT and north-central WY continues\nto remain in effect.\n\nRelatively low amount of cooler air is brought in with this\nsystem, only dropping snow levels at around 10,000 ft. So \nelevations above this will see 1-4 inches of snow.\n\nAs the upper low shifts through Wyoming on Sunday, we will still\nreceive additional rain from the wrap-around. However, little\ninstability exists during this period, so the chance for localized\nareas of heavy rain with rain rates of 2-3\" per hour is much\nlower. The only exception (while still being a lower chance than\ntoday) is areas at and south of US-212 from Crow Agency through \nAlzada, where CAPE up to 200 J/kg is forecasted to occur. All rain\nis expected to shift east of Montana by midnight Sunday night.\n\nAs we enter the work week, expect a quick warm-up to slightly\nabove normal temperatures and mainly dry conditions throughout the\nweek. A week shortwave is currently progged to cross southern MT\non Wednesday, bringing with it a small (less than 20%) chance of\nshowers and storms to the area. Vertz",
